Defer Tracy's Linux kernel-symbol scan until a profiler attaches.

WHY THIS IS A PATCH
    Tracy's `InitCallstack` runs on the symbol worker at process startup, even
    with `TRACY_ON_DEMAND`. On Linux it eagerly reads and parses
    `/proc/kallsyms`, which is unrelated to user-space callstack capture and
    symbolisation. On the server this work dominates an otherwise idle launch.

WHAT STAYS AVAILABLE
    `InitCallstack` still creates libbacktrace state, image caches and the
    demangling buffer before any connection. The kernel-symbol cache is loaded
    immediately before Tracy publishes a successful connection and installs its
    crash handler. A connected capture, including a crash report, therefore has
    the same kernel-symbol lookup as upstream. A process without an attached
    profiler does no kernel scan.

WHY IT LIVES HERE
    `mono.vendor/AGENTS.md` requires changes to linked vendor code to be a
    visible patch applied to an archived copy. This is Linux-specific engine
    startup policy, so carrying it locally is more appropriate than changing a
    generic upstream default.

WHERE IT GOES
    `InitKernelSymbols` remains in `TracyCallstack.cpp` but becomes callable
    from `TracyProfiler.cpp`. The profiler worker calls it after its on-demand
    connection bookkeeping and before `m_isConnected` or the crash handler.
    The boolean makes reconnects a no-op.

WHEN IT STOPS APPLYING
    `scripts/vendor-tree.sh tracy` fails during configure. Re-point these three
    hunks at Tracy's callstack declaration, Linux kernel cache, and connection
    publication sites. Do not edit `mono.vendor/tracy` in place.

